From 83f9f802b9e0fdd2922138b17b8af0ebb0db9738 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:49:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayerVideo: a frame takes the picture subtitles that
 start nearer its time than the next frame's

ProcessOverlays() showed an overlay from the first frame whose time is at or after its start. A
picture subtitle (PGS, DVB, DVD) is made for a frame, but Matroska keeps every time to the
millisecond, the frame's and the subtitle's each rounded its own way, so a set made for a frame can
start a millisecond or two after it. Such a set showed a frame late, and its end a frame late; a set
that the next one follows on the next frame never showed. In a remux of a UHD Blu-ray (Tron: Legacy,
its 42 PGS tracks), 137 of 2898 sets started on the frame after their own, up to 8 of 76 in a track.

A frame now takes its overlays at its middle: its time plus half its duration
(OverlayFrameMatch.h). Half a frame cannot take a set that starts on the next frame, so a set made
for a frame keeps it; a start that lies between two frames goes to the nearer one instead of always
to the later one; a frame without a duration keeps its own time. The container is cleaned up at the
same time, the subtitle delay still moves the sets by itself, forced sets are matched alike, and the
render manager still gets the frame's own time. Text subtitles are not affected: their overlay
covers the whole file and libass places each line by the frame's time.

The idea and the form of the change are matthane's, for CoreELEC's Kodi ("VideoPlayer: match
subtitle overlays at the middle of the frame").
